a class="type-ui transition-colors duration-200 font-normal text-ink-muted hover:text-ink" href="/sell/">Sell</a><a class="type-ui transition-colors duration-200 font-normal text-ink-muted hover:text-ink" href="/calculator/">Calculator</a><a class="type-ui px-5 py-2 rounded-full bg-brand text-white hover:bg-brand-hover transition-colors duration-200" href="/apply/">Request a free catalog review</a></div><button class="md:hidden p-2 -mr-1 text-ink-muted hover:text-ink transition-colors" aria-label="Open menu"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-menu h-[18px] w-[18px]"><line x1="4" x2="20" y1="12" y2="12"></line><line x1="4" x2="20" y1="6" y2="6"></line><line x1="4" x2="20" y1="18" y2="18"></line></svg></button></div></div><div class="md:hidden overflow-hidden transition-all duration-300 ease-out max-h-0 opacity-0"><div class="border-t border-black/[0.08] bg-cream/98 backdrop-blur-2xl px-6 py-5"><div class="flex flex-col gap-0.5"><a class="type-ui py-3.5 transition-colors font-normal text-ink-muted hover:text-ink" href="/how-it-works/">How It Works</a><a class="type-ui py-3.5 transition-colors font-normal text-ink-muted hover:text-ink" href="/sell/">Sell</a><a class="type-ui py-3.5 transition-colors font-normal text-ink-muted hover:text-ink" href="/calculator/">Calculator</a><div class="pt-4 mt-3 border-t border-black/[0.08] space-y-2"><a class="type-ui block text-center px-5 py-2.5 rounded-full bg-brand text-white hover:bg-brand-hover transition-colors" href="/apply/">Request a free catalog review</a></div></div></div></div></nav><main class="space-blog-article"><article><header class="space-blog-header"><nav aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">SPACE</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span>Catalog Buyer Comparisons</span></nav><h1>Companies That Buy Ambient and Lo-Fi Music Catalogs: A Comparison</h1><p class="space-blog-meta">By <a href="/about/">SPACE</a> | <time datetime="2026-09-07">September 7, 2026</time> | 10 min read</p></header><div class="space-blog-content"><p>If you want to sell an ambient or lo-fi music catalog, start by comparing the transaction, not just the company name. SPACE offers direct catalog purchases; Duetti and Limbo also publish acquisition options. Royalty Exchange connects sellers with investors, while beatBread offers royalty advances rather than a conventional catalog buyout. Your rights, earnings and preferred deal structure determine which conversation is relevant.</p>
<p><strong>Disclosure:</strong> SPACE publishes this guide and is one of the companies included. This is not an independent ranking or a test of private offers. We compare publicly described services, identify questions to ask and do not claim that any company will accept your catalog or pay the highest price. Sources were checked on September 7, 2026.</p>
</div><nav class="space-blog-toc" aria-label="In this article"><p>In this article</p><ol><li><a href="#catalog-buyers-and-funding-options-at-a-glance">Catalog buyers and funding options at a glance</a></li><li><a href="#what-does-each-company-offer">What does each company offer?</a></li><li><a href="#what-matters-specifically-for-ambient-and-lo-fi-catalogs">What matters specifically for ambient and lo-fi catalogs?</a></li><li><a href="#how-to-compare-offers-on-equal-terms">How to compare offers on equal terms</a></li><li><a href="#compare-the-offer-date-with-the-payment-date">Compare the offer date with the payment date</a></li><li><a href="#which-route-should-you-explore-first">Which route should you explore first?</a></li><li><a href="#frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</a></li><li><a href="#start-with-a-defined-catalog-not-a-promised-multiple">Start with a defined catalog, not a promised multiple</a></li></ol></nav><div class="space-blog-content"><h2 id="catalog-buyers-and-funding-options-at-a-glance">Catalog buyers and funding options at a glance</h2>
<p>The table separates potential acquisition routes from funding alternatives. It does not imply that every company specializes in ambient or lo-fi, or that an advance transfers the underlying copyright.</p>
<div class="space-blog-table" role="region" aria-label="Article comparison table" tabindex="0"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Company</th>
<th>Publicly described model</th>
<th>First question to ask</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><a href="/pricing.md">SPACE</a></td>
<td>Direct catalog purchases</td>
<td>What rights, share and documented earnings would the offer cover?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><a href="https://www.duetti.co/what-we-do">Duetti</a></td>
<td>Purchases of masters, publishing and royalty interests</td>
<td>Which interest qualifies, and what income and services would I retain?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><a href="https://www.limbomusic.com/funding">Limbo</a></td>
<td>Catalog sales with investor partners; royalty advances</td>
<td>Am I discussing a sale or an advance, and is distribution with Limbo required?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><a href="https://royaltyexchange.com/how-auctions-work-sellers">Royalty Exchange</a></td>
<td>Marketplace connecting royalty sellers and investors</td>
<td>What asset is listed, what information is disclosed and what fee applies?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><a href="https://www.beatbread.com/faq">beatBread</a></td>
<td>Advances structured as limited revenue purchases</td>
<td>What is the recoupment share, term, cost and treatment of future releases?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><a href="https://primarywave.com/about/">Primary Wave</a></td>
<td>Catalog acquisitions and artist or estate partnerships</td>
<td>Does my catalog fit its acquisition and partnership strategy?</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Primary Wave is included for wider market context, not as a verified small-catalog application route. The other entries offer more directly described catalog-sale or funding processes. Inclusion is not confirmation of genre-specific eligibility.</p>
<h2 id="what-does-each-company-offer">What does each company offer?</h2>
<h3 id="space-a-direct-catalog-review">SPACE: a direct catalog review</h3>
<p>SPACE buys catalogs directly from independent artists, producers, labels and owners. There is no application charge, listing fee or auction commission. The review considers the rights and revenue evidence, and sellers are not obliged to accept an offer. <a href="/pricing.md">SPACE's offer policy</a>.</p>
<p>An initial offer typically takes <strong>5-7 business days after the relevant catalog information is received</strong>, subject to review. Payment follows completion of the agreement and transfer requirements; that review estimate is not a guaranteed payment deadline. <a href="/pricing.md">Timing and payment policy</a>.</p>
<p><strong>When to consider it:</strong> You want a private purchase discussion rather than running an auction. For the genre-specific application context, see <a href="/sell/sell-ambient-music-catalog/">selling an ambient catalog</a> or <a href="/sell/sell-lo-fi-beats-catalog/">selling a lo-fi beats catalog</a>.</p>
<p><strong>What to check:</strong> Ask which recordings, ownership shares and income streams are included. A direct process does not guarantee an offer, a premium price or the absence of continuing contractual obligations.</p>
<h3 id="duetti-acquisitions-for-independent-music-creators">Duetti: acquisitions for independent music creators</h3>
<p>Duetti publicly offers purchases of master, publishing and royalty interests, alongside catalog management and marketing. Its website also describes deals for contributors with royalty income who do not control the master. <a href="https://www.duetti.co/what-we-do">Duetti's services and purchase scope</a>.</p>
<p><strong>When to consider it:</strong> You want to discuss an acquisition and the management of the catalog after the transaction.</p>
<p><strong>What to check:</strong> Confirm the interest being purchased, any retained income, required distribution arrangements and which services form part of the signed agreement. Published services are not a guarantee of higher future royalties or acceptance of your catalog.</p>
<h3 id="limbo-sales-and-advances-are-separate-routes">Limbo: sales and advances are separate routes</h3>
<p>Limbo describes catalog sales covering masters, publishing, or both, from individual tracks to full or partial interests. It says it works with professional investor partners, rather than describing itself simply as a standalone acquisition fund. <a href="https://www.limbomusic.com/sell-your-music-catalog">Limbo's catalog-sale page</a>.</p>
<p>Its advance offering has an important qualification: <strong>Limbo says royalty advances are exclusive to its distribution clients</strong>. The advance is recouped from a share of royalties, with terms and cost agreed per deal. <a href="https://www.limbomusic.com/royalty-advances">Limbo's advance conditions</a>.</p>
<p><strong>When to consider it:</strong> You want to explore sale structures, or you already distribute through Limbo and want to discuss an advance.</p>
<p><strong>What to check:</strong> Identify the contracting purchaser or funding party. Ask whether distribution migration, administration or other arrangements are required for the specific product you are considering. An illustrative multiple on a website is not your offer.</p>
<h3 id="royalty-exchange-a-marketplace-not-one-direct-buyer">Royalty Exchange: a marketplace, not one direct buyer</h3>
<p>Royalty Exchange's auction process allows a seller to define the royalty asset and minimum starting price. If that price is not met, no sale occurs. The listing agreement identifies the asset and platform fee, and the process includes verification of royalty records. <a href="https://royaltyexchange.com/how-auctions-work-sellers">Royalty Exchange's seller process</a>.</p>
<p><strong>When to consider it:</strong> You want to test investor demand through a marketplace rather than negotiate only with a single purchaser.</p>
<p><strong>What to check:</strong> Ask what appears in the listing, who can access earnings information, what the fee covers and when payment becomes available. Competitive bidding may help, but there is no basis here for predicting that it will outperform or underperform a private offer for your catalog.</p>
<h3 id="beatbread-funding-against-defined-future-revenues">beatBread: funding against defined future revenues</h3>
<p><strong>beatBread says its advances are not loans</strong> and describes them as purchases of a limited portion of revenues for a limited time. It charges fees but says it does not charge interest. Its FAQ also explains that collections can continue beyond the term if the advance has not recouped. <a href="https://www.beatbread.com/faq">beatBread's FAQ</a>.</p>
<p><strong>When to consider it:</strong> You want capital and are willing to share future revenues rather than pursue a conventional full catalog sale.</p>
<p><strong>What to check:</strong> Compare the amount advanced, fees, retained income, included tracks, future-release commitments and recoupment conditions. Do not assume that every advance is cost-free, that all royalties are withheld, or that all providers use the same legal structure.</p>
<h3 id="primary-wave-acquisition-and-partnership-context">Primary Wave: acquisition and partnership context</h3>
<p>Primary Wave's public positioning emphasizes iconic catalogs and artist or estate partnerships, with investment, marketing and catalog development. Its history includes varied rights transactions, not just one standardized form of buyout. <a href="https://primarywave.com/about/">Primary Wave's company overview</a>.</p>
<p><strong>When to consider it:</strong> You are assessing whether a broader catalog or artist partnership fits its strategy, potentially with professional representation.</p>
<p><strong>What to check:</strong> Establish acquisition fit before using major public deals as a valuation comparison. We have not verified a universal minimum deal size or a rule excluding all independent, ambient or lo-fi catalogs. We therefore do not label it either universally accessible or categorically unavailable.</p>
<h2 id="what-matters-specifically-for-ambient-and-lo-fi-catalogs">What matters specifically for ambient and lo-fi catalogs?</h2>
<p>A catalog used for study, sleep or meditation should be assessed on its own records. A genre label does not prove predictable earnings, and a long track list does not establish diversification if a handful of recordings generates most of the income.</p>
<p>Prepare evidence that helps explain:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Income concentration:</strong> Which tracks and payers account for the revenue, and what happens if a major source declines?</li>
<li><strong>Revenue history:</strong> Do the statements show recurring earnings, seasonal changes or a short-lived spike?</li>
<li><strong>Licensing income:</strong> Are wellness-app or other direct-license receipts supported by agreements, and are they recurring, one-off or restricted?</li>
<li><strong>Rights available:</strong> Do samples, collaborators, labels or existing exclusive licenses limit what can be sold?</li>
<li><strong>Adjustments and disputes:</strong> Are there deductions, withheld amounts or unresolved royalty issues that a buyer needs to understand?</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not treat a public stream count as equivalent to cash received. Spotify says confirmed artificial streaming can lead to withheld royalties and corrected public counts, and warns against paid third parties guaranteeing streams or playlist placement. <a href="https://support.spotify.com/us/artists/article/third-party-services-that-guarantee-streams/">Spotify's artificial-streaming guidance</a>.</p>
<p>Genre familiarity can make the conversation more relevant. It does not establish that a specialist will always pay more than a diversified buyer. Ask each counterparty how it evaluates the documented income and risks rather than assuming its branding predicts the price.</p>
<h2 id="how-to-compare-offers-on-equal-terms">How to compare offers on equal terms</h2>
<p>Give each prospective buyer the same proposed rights scope and the same revenue records. Then compare the written responses using a consistent checklist:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Rights and ownership share:</strong> Masters, publishing or a royalty-income interest? Selected tracks or the full catalog? What stays yours?</li>
<li><strong>Earnings basis:</strong> Gross royalties, net recording income and net publisher's share are not interchangeable. Which figure and accounting period does the offer use?</li>
<li><strong>Net payment:</strong> What reaches you at closing after stated deductions, fees, balances to settle and any holdback?</li>
<li><strong>Duration and control:</strong> How long does the arrangement last, and who can authorize licensing, distribution changes or other uses?</li>
<li><strong>Future work and obligations:</strong> Are new recordings, delivery commitments, options or ongoing cooperation included?</li>
<li><strong>Closing conditions:</strong> What verification, signatures, consents and transfer steps must happen before payment?</li>
</ol>
<p>For worked arithmetic and a fuller net-proceeds checklist, read our <a href="/blog/sell-music-catalog-lump-sum-2026/">lump-sum catalog sale guide</a>. It deliberately labels sample multiples as assumptions rather than market offers.</p>
<p>Before committing, have independent legal and tax advisers review the actual agreement. This comparison is general information, not personalized financial, tax or legal advice.</p>
<h2 id="compare-the-offer-date-with-the-payment-date">Compare the offer date with the payment date</h2>
<p>An application response, a conditional offer and money available in your bank account are different milestones. Ask each provider for a timeline that separates them rather than comparing an advertised review period with another company's full closing process.</p>
<div class="space-blog-table" role="region" aria-label="Article comparison table" tabindex="0"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Milestone</th>
<th>What to confirm</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>Review and indicative offer</td>
<td>When does the clock start, and which documents are still required?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Signed agreement</td>
<td>Is the price final, and what approvals or transfer conditions remain?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Funds available to you</td>
<td>Is payment subject to escrow release, delivery, verification or a holdback?</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>A direct buyer avoids an auction stage, but that alone does not prove it will pay sooner. Royalty Exchange's seller process, for example, releases funds after the transfer is complete, not simply when bidding ends. <a href="https://royaltyexchange.com/how-auctions-work-sellers">Royalty Exchange's closing process</a>.</p>
<h3 id="confirm-fees-and-continuing-obligations">Confirm fees and continuing obligations</h3>
<p>Request an itemized explanation of the costs payable at application, listing, withdrawal and closing. Do not assume an auction charges the same fees whether or not an asset sells, or that a free application makes every eventual transaction cost disappear. Compare net proceeds after the deductions that actually apply to your agreement.</p>
<p>A sale is not automatically a release from every obligation. Check ownership warranties, cooperation requirements, licensing approvals and the reporting needed for any interest you retain. An upfront payment describes when money arrives; it does not, by itself, establish the duration or scope of the deal.</p>
<h2 id="which-route-should-you-explore-first">Which route should you explore first?</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>You are willing to transfer a defined interest:</strong> Request comparable purchase proposals from relevant buyers. Compare net proceeds and retained rights, not just speed.</li>
<li><strong>You want to test investor demand:</strong> Ask a marketplace about its listing process, disclosure rules, fees and minimum price before agreeing to list.</li>
<li><strong>You want funding while retaining underlying ownership:</strong> Examine an advance provider's revenue-sharing and recoupment terms, including any distribution requirements.</li>
<li><strong>You are unsure whether to sell:</strong> Start with the rights and cash-flow decision. Keeping the catalog, selling selected tracks or transferring a smaller interest may meet different goals.</li>
</ul>
<p>No route is automatically best for every producer. Our <a href="/compare/partial-sale-vs-full-sale/">partial versus full sale comparison</a> can help you define the interest you want reviewed.</p>
<h2 id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3 id="which-company-pays-the-most-for-ambient-or-lo-fi-catalogs">Which company pays the most for ambient or lo-fi catalogs?</h3>
<p>This guide cannot establish a highest-paying company from public marketing pages. Compare actual offers for the same rights and income. A higher headline price may include a larger interest, a longer term or less favorable conditions.</p>
<h3 id="are-all-the-companies-listed-direct-catalog-buyers">Are all the companies listed direct catalog buyers?</h3>
<p>No. Royalty Exchange operates a marketplace, while beatBread's described funding product is an advance structured as a revenue purchase. Limbo offers both sales and advances with different conditions. Confirm the counterparty and transaction before comparing proposals.</p>
<h3 id="does-a-non-recourse-advance-mean-there-are-no-obligations">Does a non-recourse advance mean there are no obligations?</h3>
<p>No. Ask the provider to distinguish what happens when royalties underperform from what happens if a contractual promise is breached. Review the revenue-sharing term, fees, reporting and any exceptions with your adviser. Also confirm whether collections end at recoupment, at the end of the term, or only when both conditions are met; do not assume these are the same date.</p>
<h3 id="is-there-a-standard-ambient-catalog-valuation-multiple">Is there a standard ambient catalog valuation multiple?</h3>
<p>We have not established a universal genre-specific market range. A multiple only becomes comparable once the earnings definition, ownership share, term and included rights match. Website illustrations and celebrity acquisitions do not establish an offer for your catalog.</p>
<h3 id="can-i-keep-publishing-rights-when-i-sell-my-masters">Can I keep publishing rights when I sell my masters?</h3>
<p>Yes, if you own those publishing interests and the transaction leaves them outside the sale. Recording and composition rights are separate. Make the retained rights explicit; our <a href="/compare/selling-masters-vs-selling-publishing/">masters versus publishing comparison</a> explains the distinction.</p>
<h3 id="does-selling-existing-tracks-include-my-future-releases">Does selling existing tracks include my future releases?</h3>
<p>Not if the agreement is expressly limited to specified existing works and contains no relevant future-release commitment or option. Do not rely on a marketing summary: check the contract's scope before assuming future music is excluded.</p>
<h2 id="start-with-a-defined-catalog-not-a-promised-multiple">Start with a defined catalog, not a promised multiple</h2>
<p>Gather your royalty statements and ownership records, decide what you would consider selling, and ask for the terms in writing. If you want SPACE to review your catalog, <a href="/apply/">apply for a free catalog evaluation</a>. You can also <a href="/calculator/">estimate your catalog value</a> as an indicative starting point, not a guaranteed offer.</p>
